# Changelog (engine/v1/)

## Camera obstruction broadphase

**What:** `checkCameraObstruction` now runs `BroadphaseCollectCandidates` over an AABB spanning the camera boom segment instead of walking terrain, obstacles and void walls by hand. `BroadphaseCollectCandidates` was promoted from module-private to exported.

**Why:** It was the only engine site running triangle-soup narrowphase against every level object every frame with no broadphase reject, despite a boom only a few CNU long.

**Where:**
- `engine/v1/physics/Collision.js:1074` — added `BroadphaseCollectCandidates` to the export block.
- `engine/v1/handlers/game/Camera.js:20` — new import from `physics/Collision.js`.
- `engine/v1/handlers/game/Camera.js:343-389` — `checkCameraObstruction` rewritten: local boom AABB, single candidate loop, per-type narrowphase dispatch (`voidWall` keeps the `RayAABBIntersect` gate + direct `RayDetailedBoundsIntersect`; terrain/obstacle keep `RayAABBDetailedBoundsIntersect`).

## Per-mesh render matrix cache

**What:** `CreateRenderMatrix(mesh.displayTransform)` is no longer recomputed per mesh per frame. Each mesh carries a `renderMatrixCache` — a `Float32Array` holding the matrix plus a by-value snapshot of the four transform fields it was built from. `renderMatrixFor(mesh)` compares the snapshot and returns the cached array unchanged on a hit; on a miss it writes into the existing `Float32Array` via `.set()` and updates the snapshot in place. Zero allocation on the hot path.

**Why:** The matrix was rebuilt for every mesh every frame, and up to three times per frame for the same mesh — a decal-bearing terrain mesh in a level with voids passes through `drawDepthPrePass`, PASS A and `drawDecalPass`. Each call allocated twice via `toWorldUnit()`, once via `ScaleVector3`, ran trig, returned a fresh array, and was then wrapped in a fresh `Float32Array`.

**Where:**
- `engine/v1/math/Vector3.js:74` — `vector3Matches` hoisted out of `builder/NewEntity.js` as exported `Vector3Matches`; needed in both `builder/` and `handlers/`, so it belongs in the shared layer. No duplicate remains.
- `engine/v1/math/Matrix.js:112-120` — new exported `CreateRenderMatrixCache(transform)`, a pure factory. Placed next to `CreateRenderMatrix` deliberately: the snapshot's field set must stay in lockstep with what that function reads, and colocating makes the invariant local to one file rather than an argument spread across three.
- `engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js:1145`, `:1213` and `engine/v1/builder/NewVoid.js:222`, `:267` — the four mesh literals carrying `displayTransform` now populate `renderMatrixCache` at construction. Fully populated there, never lazily, so `Render.js` needs no existence guard.
- `engine/v1/handlers/Render.js:1193-1211` — `copyVector3Into` and `renderMatrixFor`; call sites at `:1224`, `:1589`, `:1777`, `:1902`.

**Invariant this rests on:** a mesh has `displayTransform` if and only if it has `renderMatrixCache`. The `"invisible"` and `"void"` literals at `NewObject.js:1072` and `:1102` have neither, and every consumer filters to `mode === "default"` upstream — those meshes would have crashed the previous `CreateRenderMatrix(mesh.displayTransform)` path identically, so the change alters no risk.

**Why by-value and not by-reference:** `writePartDisplay` (`handlers/game/Animation.js:142-157`) creates the display object once and mutates it in place thereafter, so reference identity is stable while values change. A reference compare would silently go stale. This mirrors `poseMatchesTransform` (`builder/NewEntity.js:139-143`), which already solved the same problem the same way.

**Correctness note on units:** `CreateRenderMatrix` reads `position`/`pivot` through `toWorldUnit()`, which is `.type`-dependent, so a `.type` flip with unchanged `x/y/z` would desync the cache. Every in-place unit-mutating call (`toWorldUnit(true)`, `toCNU(true)`, `toRadians(true)`, `toDegrees(true)`) is confined to `core/normalize.js` and `builder/templates/Instance.js` and never touches a transform attached to a live mesh, so `.type` is fixed at instancing and the four component vectors fully determine the matrix.

`builder/NewScatter.js:440` is deliberately untouched — build-time, on a synthetic transform literal, not a mesh.

## Narrowphase cap in RayAABBDetailedBoundsIntersect

**What:** `RayAABBDetailedBoundsIntersect` now passes the caller's `maxDistance` to the narrowphase instead of `broadHit.t`.

**Why:** It was passing the broad AABB's *entry* distance as the narrowphase's limit. Detailed bounds sit inside the AABB, so a genuine narrowphase hit is always at or beyond the broad entry — an entry-t cap is structurally incapable of admitting a hit and can only ever reject one. Symptom: the camera treated a multi-part obstacle as if it had no collision at all and flew straight through it.

**Mechanism:** triangle-soup triangles lie strictly inside the object's broad AABB, so every triangle hit is at a `t` greater than the AABB entry distance. Capping the narrowphase at that entry rejected all of them, on every frame — a deterministic total failure, matching the observed behaviour exactly.

**Why only that obstacle:** `math/Collision.js:804` short-circuits `detailedBounds.type === "aabb"` and returns the broad hit before reaching the capped narrowphase, so every AABB-shaped object dodged this. The two obstacles in testGame that declare `collisionShape` declare `"aabb"`. The rock obstacle (`platform-b`, cube root with `rock-1`/`rock-2` sphere parts) declares none — and `core/normalize.js:1157` passes `"triangle-soup"` as the multipart fallback for obstacles, so any obstacle with more than one part and no authored `collisionShape` resolves to triangle-soup before the builder ever runs (`core/normalize.js:891-893`). `CreateDetailedBoundsFromParts` then merges the parts' triangles into true per-part bounds — and onto the capped narrowphase path.

**Note for anyone reading `collisionShape` defaults:** the `levelObject` schema's fallback is `null` (`core/canonSchemas.json:1673`), but normalization always resolves it to a concrete shape before the builder sees it. Reading the schema alone gives the wrong answer, and `CreateDetailedBoundsFromParts`'s `createEnvelopeObb` tail (`builder/NewObstacle.js:73`) is therefore only reachable for a multi-part object whose fallback chain supplies a non-triangle-soup shape — not for ordinary multi-part obstacles.

**Where:** `engine/v1/math/Collision.js:805`.

**Prior art:** the void-wall branch of `checkCameraObstruction` already carried a comment describing this exact defect — it calls `RayDetailedBoundsIntersect` directly with "no exit-t cap rejecting boundary tris". Void walls are triangle-soup too, which is why that path hit the bug first. It was correctly diagnosed then, but patched at one call site rather than at the cause, leaving every other triangle-soup object broken.

## Ray hits report `inside` (exit-hit) state

**What:** Every ray-hit result in `math/Collision.js` now carries an `inside` flag marking an exit hit — one where the ray began within the volume, so no surface faces the origin. `checkCameraObstruction` skips flagged hits.

**Why:** `RayAABBIntersect` returns the *exit* distance when the origin is inside the box (`math/Collision.js:660-667`), not zero and not a miss. With the player's head inside an obstacle's merged bounds, that exit distance was a positive `t` clearing the `hit.t > 0` guard, and the `Math.max(obstructionMinDistance, closestT - obstructionOffset)` clamp then pinned the result to exactly `0.50` every time. The camera was reading "my own origin is inside geometry" as "something is between me and the camera."

**Where:**
- `engine/v1/math/Collision.js` — `noRayHit` and `RayTriangleIntersect` report `false` (a triangle has no interior); `RayAABBIntersect` sets it in the origin-inside branch; `RaySphereIntersect` had the same latent shape (`let t = -b - root; if (t < 0) t = -b + root;` silently falls through to the exit root) and that fallback is now the flag; `RayCapsuleIntersect`'s body roots set it when the far root is reached only because the near one is behind the origin, its end caps inheriting from the sphere path; `RayOBBIntersect` propagates it.
- `engine/v1/handlers/game/Camera.js:377` — the candidate loop skips flagged hits.

**Contract note:** the flag is present on every constructor rather than only where it can be true, so consumers read a guaranteed field instead of a possibly-absent one. `limitRayHit` and `chooseClosestRayHit` pass whole result objects through, so it survives the triangle-soup and compound paths unchanged.

**Scope:** physics is unaffected — the change is purely additive, no existing `t` or `normal` value changed, and physics reads only `.hit`/`.t`/`.normal`. Void walls are structurally unaffected: that camera branch discards the broad `t` and uses only triangle-soup results, which never set the flag. The sphere and capsule paths are not exercisable by current testGame content and were accepted on inspection rather than tested.

**Rejected alternative:** a camera-local test for the origin being inside the candidate's AABB, touching no shared math. It over-rejects when the head is inside a bounding box but outside the actual solid — a sphere's box corner, say — which would reintroduce the same silent-no-obstruction failure class the entry above had just fixed.

## DefaultCam horizontal foreshortening restored

**What:** `desiredPos` in `updateDefaultCamState` again applies `cos(pitch)` to its X and Z components.

**Why:** A refactor of `Camera.js` dropped that factor, leaving `x = d·sin(yaw)` and `z = d·cos(yaw)`. `y` was untouched, so the error was invisible at pitch 0 and grew with pitch — roughly 3% at the default −15°, 32% at the −60° clamp. Two effects compounded. `desiredPos` left the sphere (its distance from `targetPoint` became `d·√(1 + sin²pitch)`), so the obstruction ray both overshot and pointed along a different line than the real boom; and `finalPos` still used the correct spherical formula, so a `clippedDistance` measured along the longer ray was applied as the radius in the correct one — placing the camera farther out than the obstruction it had just detected. Symptom: obstruction registered against the rock obstacle, but the camera still entered it.

**Where:** `engine/v1/handlers/game/Camera.js` — `updateDefaultCamState`, the `desiredPos` construction.

**Load-bearing relationship:** `desiredPos` and `finalPos` must describe the same sphere around `targetPoint`. `clippedDistance` is measured along the ray to `desiredPos` and then consumed as the radius by `finalPos`; if the two formulas disagree, obstruction distances silently stop meaning what the placement uses them for.
